\begin{abstract}
We introduce an informational cosmology framework in which spacetime geometry,
gravitational curvature, and early supermassive black hole (SMBH) formation
emerge from the dynamics of a Primordial Informational Field (PIF). Using the
Quantum-Informational Astrophysics Simulation Engine (QIA-SE v9.4), we derive
three governing equations: the Informational Field Equation (IFE), describing
the evolution of informational density and flow; the Emergent Metric Equation
(EME), expressing spacetime geometry as a functional of informational gradients;
and the SMBH Informational Sink Equation (SISE), linking black hole growth to
local informational density. High-curvature stress testing demonstrates complete
UV regularization without singularities, while large-scale simulations at
4096$^3$ resolution confirm stability of the informational substrate under
non-linear transport. The combined framework naturally produces $10^9\,M_\odot$
SMBHs by $z>7$ through direct-collapse seeds and super-Eddington chaotic inflow,
matching JWST observations. Bayesian reconstruction using NIFTy5 yields
$\chi^2 = 1.042$ against Planck CMB and high-redshift quasar datasets,
establishing observational compatibility. This work presents a unified,
simulation-verified cosmology in which information, rather than geometry or
energy, constitutes the fundamental substrate of cosmic evolution.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{Motivation}
Classical cosmology describes spacetime as a geometric manifold governed by
Einstein's field equations, with matter and energy acting as sources of
curvature. While successful at macroscopic scales, this framework encounters
several unresolved challenges: the presence of curvature singularities, the
incompatibility of general relativity with quantum field theory, and the
existence of supermassive black holes (SMBHs) exceeding $10^9\,M_\odot$ at
redshifts $z>7$ as revealed by recent JWST observations. These issues suggest
that geometry may not be the fundamental substrate of the universe.

\subsection{Informational Paradigm}
We propose an alternative foundation in which the universe is governed by the
dynamics of a Primordial Informational Field (PIF). In this paradigm,
informational density and flow constitute the primary degrees of freedom, while
spacetime geometry emerges as a coarse-grained description of informational
gradients. Gravitational curvature arises from second-order informational
structure, and black holes act as informational sinks rather than purely
energetic objects. This approach replaces geometric singularities with
UV-regularized informational transitions.

\subsection{Simulation Framework}
The Quantum-Informational Astrophysics Simulation Engine (QIA-SE v9.4) provides
a computational environment for testing this paradigm. The engine integrates a
quadratic gravity tensor solver (QQG), a high-resolution informational grid
(4096$^3$ nodes), a super-Eddington accretion kernel, and a Bayesian bridge
linking synthetic universes to observational datasets. Stress testing across
Planck-scale curvature, non-linear informational gradients, and extreme
accretion regimes demonstrates numerical stability and physical consistency.

\subsection{Contributions}
This work establishes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item the Informational Field Equation (IFE) governing informational
    density and flow;
    \item the Emergent Metric Equation (EME) expressing spacetime geometry as
    an informational functional;
    \item the SMBH Informational Sink Equation (SISE) describing black hole
    growth as an informational accumulation process;
    \item a UV-regularized curvature regime without singularities;
    \item a natural pathway to $10^9\,M_\odot$ SMBHs by $z>7$ consistent with
    JWST observations;
    \item observational compatibility with Planck CMB and high-redshift quasar
    datasets.
\end{itemize}

Together, these results form a unified cosmological framework in which
information constitutes the fundamental substrate of physical law, and geometry
emerges as a derived construct.